== Description
<description-1>
#emph[Requested by #strong[Sia] ·
#link("https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2GSdfx2LDjjW1ejiT3SruTH")[project thread]]

Before: the modules in
#raw(lang:"verbatim", ".config/emacs-modular/libraries/modules/")
existed only as #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".el") files, and most had no
documentation.

After: #raw(lang:"verbatim", "docs/tangles/") has a canonical
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-*.org") file for each of the 127 modules. Each
one holds the module\'s full source, in order, in
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs-lisp") blocks, with headings and prose
explaining every block. Tangling a doc
(#raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-c C-v t")) regenerates its module.

#strong[How]

- Each file sets
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "#+PROPERTY: header-args:emacs-lisp :tangle ../../libraries/modules/\u{3c}category>/\u{3c}module>.el :comments no :padline yes :noweb no"),
  so tangling rewrites the real module file. Nothing tangles unless you
  run it, because no file sets #raw(lang:"verbatim", "auto_tangle").

- The code was copied by a script, not retyped. Blocks are split only at
  single blank lines, so #raw(lang:"verbatim", ":padline yes") restores
  each original blank line. Lines starting with
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "*") or #raw(lang:"verbatim", "#+") are
  comma-escaped. Blocks use #raw(lang:"verbatim", "-i") and start at
  column 0, so indentation survives whatever
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "org-src-preserve-indentation") is set to.

- 14 modules already had an older doc under the same name, for example
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-faces.org"). Those older docs are renamed to
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{3c}name>-old.org") with their contents unchanged,
  and the new docs take the plain name.

- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "lem-dashboard.el") gets
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-lem-dashboard.org").

- Third-party code under
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "libraries/modules/cloned/") is not included.

- Five modules are empty \.el files:
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syslinux"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-instant-messenger"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-mail"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-music-comp") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-vulpea-ext-back"). Their docs are literate
  skeletons with these parts:

  - a file header
  - a Configuration section to fill in
  - a #raw(lang:"verbatim", ":tangle no") Sandbox for trying settings
  - a #raw(lang:"verbatim", "provide") form

  Tangling one of these writes a valid module that loads, replacing the
  empty file.

- Commits: there is one commit per module category, then one for the
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "-old") renames and one for the empty-module
  skeletons.

#strong[Round-trip check] (Emacs 29.3 / Org 9.6, tangled into a scratch
copy and compared with #raw(lang:"verbatim", "cmp"))

- 44 modules come back byte-for-byte identical.
- 78 differ only by a final newline. Their #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".el")
  files have no newline at the end, and Org always ends a tangled file
  with one.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-org-archive.el") also loses two trailing
  spaces on its last line.
- All five skeletons tangle to modules that load in
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs -Q").

No #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".el") file is changed by this PR. Against
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "main"), 127 files are added and 14 are modified;
the modified ones are the canonical names that now hold the new docs.

