#strong[Before:] every box in the browser graph and the SVG export had
the same colour, and a click on a box folded it. The page only knew the
part of the tree that Emacs had cut, so it could not move to another
note.

#strong[After:] #raw(lang:"verbatim", "vulpea-hierarchy-tag-colors")
maps a file tag to a colour (defaults for #raw(lang:"verbatim", "root"),
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "idea"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "question"),
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "claim"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "evidence"),
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "source")). A note takes the colour of the first
of its tags that is listed, drawn as a tint, and the legend lists the
tags in view. In the browser, a click on a box recentres the graph on it
and remakes the nodes from there. A small ▾ marker folds a branch,
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "−") and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "+") change the
depth, and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "←") or Backspace goes back to the
previous centre. The SVG export uses the same colours.

#strong[How:] the page now embeds the whole tree and runs the depth cut
itself (the same breadth-first walk as
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "vulpea-hierarchy--scope")), so recentring needs
no Emacs round trip. For a very large vault this makes the page bigger,
since it holds every note of the hierarchy (ids, titles, tags and
paths). Tested on mock notes in headless Chromium: tint colours, legend,
a recentre, a depth change, back, and a JavaScript syntax check;
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "dot") accepts the tinted Graphviz source. Not run
against your real vault. The literate doc is regenerated and tangles
back byte for byte.

