== Description
<description-17>
#emph[Requested by #strong[Sia] ·
#link("https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2ApjJf3fNB4nmVud6MqNpBi")[project thread]]

Stacked on \#17 (which is stacked on \#16). Merge in order and GitHub
retargets each one.

#strong[Before:] the browser graph put boxes in columns by depth, with
parents on the left, and the other relations were drawn as lines between
boxes the tree had already placed. There was no way to say where a
relation should go.

#strong[After:] each relation in
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "vulpea-hierarchy-relations") takes a
#raw(lang:"verbatim", ":direction"): #raw(lang:"verbatim", "up"),
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "down"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "left"),
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "right"), or an angle in degrees (0 is right, 90
is up, so 45 is up and to the right). It says where the object of the
relation is drawn, seen from its subject. Parents now go above their
children, siblings sit on one row, and a note with several parents is
drawn once and joined to all of them. Defaults:
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "supports") right,
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "opposes") left, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "informs")
45, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "answers") up. The Graphviz default
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "vulpea-hierarchy-rankdir") is now
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "\"TB\"") to match; the SVG export ignores the
other directions.

#strong[Backend change (needed for this):] the page\'s own tidy-tree
code could not place a note relative to a note in another branch, so
positions now come from the fCoSE force-directed layout, which takes
\"above/below/left of/right of\" rules. The page still does its own
drawing. Pros: directions, diagonals and cross-branch rules;
multi-parent notes drawn once; deterministic. Cons: about 560 KB of
vendored MIT-licensed scripts
(#raw(lang:"verbatim", "libraries/modules/custom/vendor/vulpea-hierarchy/"),
with a README and licences) pasted into each page; heuristic layout that
breaks some rules in crowded graphs and is less tidy than a tree; about
0.5 s for 120 boxes; the scripts need manual updates; SVG export has no
directions. The full table, and the alternatives I considered (Cytoscape
renderer, ELK, d3-force, Graphviz), are in the doc section \"Layout
backend of the browser page\".

#strong[How:] #raw(lang:"verbatim", "constraints") turns the tree and
each link\'s direction into relative-placement rules, dropping any rule
that would contradict earlier ones; #raw(lang:"verbatim", "place") runs
fCoSE in a window-less Cytoscape graph;
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "separate") pushes apart boxes that still overlap.
Tested on mock notes in headless Chromium (directions, recentre, depth,
back, fold), on a 121-box tree with 25 random links (about 0.5 s,
readable but crowded), and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "dot") accepts the
Graphviz output. Not run against your real vault. The literate doc is
regenerated and tangles back byte for byte.

