Follow-up to \#24 (merged).

#strong[Before:] #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs-pr") gave all sandboxes
one shared #raw(lang:"verbatim", "elpaca") folder and one shared notes
copy, so only one sandbox could safely run at a time, and a sandbox
could only be started from a shell.

#strong[After:]

- Each PR gets its own #raw(lang:"verbatim", "var/elpaca"), an APFS
  clone of a seed that is itself a clone of the live
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "var/elpaca"), and its own
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "HOME") with a clone of
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "~/notes")
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "~/.cache/emacs-sandbox/home/pr-N")). Any
  number of sandboxes can run at once, next to the live Emacs. Only the
  native-compile cache is still shared, which Emacs supports.
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs-pr rm N") deletes everything of a PR,
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs-pr reset N") refreshes its notes, and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs-pr reseed") drops the seed.
- A new module, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-sandbox"), adds
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "M-x sp-sandbox-pr") (the number defaults to the
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "22") or #raw(lang:"verbatim", "#22") at point),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-sandbox-remove"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-sandbox-reset-notes") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-sandbox-reseed"). They run the fish function
  in a separate process from the Emacs you are working in and show its
  output in #raw(lang:"verbatim", "*emacs-pr*").
- The README section \"Testing a pull request in a sandbox\" describes
  the new layout and the commands. Chemacs2 is not needed:
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--init-directory") already gives each worktree
  its own packages, and the isolation comes from the per-PR
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "HOME") and clones, which a Chemacs2 profile
  would not provide.

#strong[How it was checked:] the function passes
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "fish -n"). I ran it against a local stand-in for
the yadm repository, with stub #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs") and
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "cp"), for two PRs, a re-run and
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "rm"). Each PR\'s build symlinks pointed into its
own #raw(lang:"verbatim", "elpaca"), each got its own home, and
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "rm") removed one without touching the other. The
Emacs commands were run in batch against a stub
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs-pr") (arguments, output buffer, number at
point), and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-sandbox.org") tangles to
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-sandbox.el") identically in Emacs 29.3. I did
not run any of this with real yadm, Emacs or APFS, so the first real
launch on the Mac is the test.

