Stacked on \#48, which is stacked on \#45. Merge in that order; GitHub
retargets each PR as its base merges.

Before: the splash colours were two fixed greys chosen for dark or light
backgrounds, plus the box face.

After: a small framework of colour roles.

- A role (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "art"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "banner"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "footer"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "accent-1..3"))
  takes its colour from a face of the current theme
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "shadow"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "font-lock-keyword-face"), …) mixed towards the
  default background, so the lambda stays quiet and the accents stay
  vivid in any theme. #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-splash-palette-roles")
  says which theme face, attribute and mix each role uses, so adding a
  role is one line.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-splash-palettes") lets you choose colours
  for a particular theme by hand; roles not listed are still derived.
- The menu boxes are coloured with the accent faces in turn
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-splash-accent-faces")), so each block has
  its own colour.
- The splash is recoloured and redrawn when a theme is enabled or
  disabled (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "enable-theme-functions"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "disable-theme-functions")\; switch off with
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-splash-palette-follow-theme")).
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-splash-apply-palette") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-splash-show-palette") do it and show it by
  hand.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-splash.org") has a new \"Colour palette\"
  section and tangles to the same text as the
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".el")\; CHANGELOG entry added.

Checked in headless Emacs 29.3 with faces set by hand: the roles came
out as derived colours, a per-theme override won, the three boxes got
the three accent faces, and enabling a test theme with a red
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "shadow") recoloured the art. Not checked: how the
derived colours look on your real themes; tune the mix values in
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-splash-palette-roles") if the art is too faint
or too strong.

