== Description
<description-98>
Follows \#97 (merged).

#strong[Before:] #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-theme-auto-mode") could only
read the macOS appearance (or the time of day);
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syswin") and
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syslinux") were not loaded, and
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syslinux") was empty.

#strong[After:]

- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-themes"):
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-theme-platform") (macos / windows / linux)
  and three alists naming the function per platform:
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-theme-system-providers") (reads light/dark),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-theme-system-watchers") (calls back on
  change, returns an unwatch function) and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-theme-ambient-providers"). A source with no
  answer, an error or nonsense falls back to the time of day. Where
  there is no watcher, the timer polls.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-macos"):
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-macos-appearance") (moved from
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-themes")) and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-macos-watch-appearance").
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syswin"):
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syswin-appearance") reads the registry value
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "AppsUseLightTheme") with
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "reg query")\; no watcher, so it is polled.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syslinux"):
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syslinux-appearance") tries the freedesktop
  portal (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "gdbus")), then
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "gsettings"), then the KDE colour scheme;
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syslinux-watch-appearance") listens to the
  portal\'s #raw(lang:"verbatim", "SettingChanged") signal with Emacs\'
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "dbus").
- Each of the three modules turns
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-theme-auto-mode") on for its own platform,
  and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "init.el") now loads
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syswin") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-syslinux").
- #strong[macOS room light:] with
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-theme-auto-source") set to
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "ambient"), the lux from
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-macos-light-sensor-command") picks a family,
  in this order from the brightest room:
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "faded-light"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "hard-light"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "faded-dark"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "hard-dark")
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-macos-ambient-levels"), default 150 / 60 /
  15 / 0 lux), and loads the first theme of that family.
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-macos-ambient-margin") (25%) keeps it from
  flickering at a boundary. Checked every 60 s
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-theme-ambient-interval")).
- 13 new ERT tests (providers, ambient, watcher, parsers, levels with
  hysteresis); all 40 tests pass on Emacs 29.3.

#strong[Not checked:] anything on macOS, Windows or a Linux desktop
(only the parsing of what they print, and the dispatch, are tested).
macOS has no command line tool for the ambient light sensor, so there is
no default #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-macos-light-sensor-command"): it
needs a command of your own that prints lux; without one,
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "ambient") follows the time of day. The default
source is still #raw(lang:"verbatim", "system").

#strong[Judgement call:] I read \"faded light, hard light, faded dark,
hard dark, in this order\" as brightest room first. If you meant another
order, reorder #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-macos-ambient-levels").

