==== Problem
<problem>
At launch a #raw(lang:"verbatim", "*scratch*") window opens beside the
Dashboard instead of the Dashboard alone.

==== Likely cause
<likely-cause>
Since 8e1917b (Oct 23) #raw(lang:"verbatim", "tabspaces-mode") starts
after init with #raw(lang:"verbatim", "tabspaces-session-auto-restore").
On startup it restores the window layout saved at the last exit
(#raw(lang:"verbatim", "window-state-put")).
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-dashboard--setup-splash-hooks") runs later, on
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "window-setup-hook"), and only replaces the buffer
of the selected window, so any restored second window (here
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "*scratch*")) stays. The layout is saved again at
every exit, so it keeps coming back. Nothing in the PRs \#114-\#131
touches this (quick-capture \#131 is not merged).

==== Fix
<fix>
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-dashboard--setup-splash-hooks") calls
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "delete-other-windows") before drawing the
Dashboard. It runs once at start-up only, not on refresh. Doc and
CHANGELOG updated.

==== Not verified
<not-verified>
Not run in Emacs (none in the sandbox); the cause is from reading the
config and the upstream #raw(lang:"verbatim", "tabspaces") source.
Please check in a fresh Emacs. If #raw(lang:"verbatim", "*scratch*")
still appears, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "M-: (window-list)") right after
launch and the contents of
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "var/tabspaces-session.eld") would show who
creates it. Under #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs --daemon") tabspaces
defers the restore to the first client frame, which runs after this
hook, so a daemon setup could still show the split.

