== Description
<description-196>
#emph[Requested by #strong[Sia] ·
#link("https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW26HKpAWZDaiUHoQ6yyQQEt8")[project thread]]

==== What
<what>
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "tools/quick-capture-native") no longer waits for
Emacs. It now:

+ asks for the line in the macOS dialog (or takes it as an argument,
  skipping the dialog),
+ writes it atomically to a spool folder
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "~/.local/state/quick-capture"), override with
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "QUICK_CAPTURE_SPOOL")),
+ pokes Emacs in the background with
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacsclient -n -e '(sp-quick-capture-drain)'")
  and returns.

#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-quick-capture-drain") (new) files the queued
lines oldest first and deletes each file. It runs on the poke, 2 seconds
after the module loads (lines captured while Emacs was down), and after
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-quick-capture-drain-interval") (30) idle
seconds. Failures go to #raw(lang:"verbatim", "failed/") in the spool.
The file\'s mtime is the capture time, used for
#raw(lang:"verbatim", ":CREATED:") and for relative dates
(#raw(lang:"verbatim", "!tom")), so a line filed days later still means
what you meant.

==== Why this approach
<why-this-approach>
Before, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacsclient -e") blocked while Emacs was
busy and the line was lost when Emacs was not running. A spool file
keeps the task safe on disk and needs no extra tool: AppleScript and
bash are already used, and Hammerspoon, Shortcuts, Alfred or Raycast can
call the script (with an argument, if they have their own input box).
Writing straight into #raw(lang:"verbatim", "inbox.org") from bash was
rejected: it would race with the open Emacs buffer and duplicate the
parser.

==== Tests
<tests>
- 4 new ERT tests (capture time, ordering, partial files ignored,
  failures parked, no spool); all 13 pass in Emacs 29.3 batch.
- Real daemon on Linux: with the daemon stopped
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "SIGSTOP")) the script returns in 0.02 s and
  the line is filed on resume. Plain
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacsclient -n") did hang for 15 s on a stopped
  daemon, which is why the poke runs in the background. With the daemon
  dead the line stays queued.
- Not run on macOS (dialog, notifications, hotkey launchers).

The module is tangled from
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "docs/tangles/text/sp-quick-capture.org")\;
CHANGELOG entry is in the Quick capture set.

