== Description
<description-227>
#emph[Requested by #strong[Sia] ·
#link("https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2DwKs8EnpZNPfWS66eBJq9A")[project thread]]

Stacked on \#228 (it moved the docs of these commands into
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-org-lint.org")); retarget to
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "main") after \#228 merges.

==== Summary
<summary>
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-author-notes") (preview, apply, refresh),
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-author-notes-bibtex"),
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-litnotes-refs-report=/")-convert= and
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-litnotes-titles-report=/")-apply= no longer
read and write the whole vault in one go. A new module,
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-async-work"), runs them as jobs in slices of
50 ms from timers, between your keystrokes (it stops a slice early when
a key is waiting).

- Progress is in the mode line, e.g.
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "[Litnote titles writing 12/340]").
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "M-x sp-async-work-cancel") stops a job; files
  already written keep their backups.
- A job waits while you type in the minibuffer, so its questions never
  replace yours.
- Reports open when the reading is done; the commands return at once.
- A big #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".bib") file is read 200 entries per
  slice.

==== Why not a second Emacs
<why-not-a-second-emacs>
The footnote lint uses #raw(lang:"verbatim", "emacs -Q --batch"), which
fits a pure file-to-result job. These commands ask questions in the
middle (disambiguating names, missing years, confirmations), query the
Vulpea database, create notes through the interactive machinery, and
update open buffers and the database after writing. A headless Emacs has
none of that, so each command would be split into two copies of its
logic. Slices keep every question and database call where it was;
nothing runs in parallel, so there is no race with your editing.

==== Safety
<safety>
- Report first, backup before write: unchanged.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-litnotes-refs-convert") now asks its (up to
  three) questions right after the report opens, before any writing, so
  nothing asks you something while you work elsewhere.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-litnotes-refs-convert") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-litnotes-titles-apply") re-check each note
  right before writing: skipped if its buffer has unsaved changes or it
  changed since the report (named in the summary). Open buffers without
  changes are reverted/killed as before.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-author-notes") keeps its earlier rule: an
  unsaved buffer is saved first when you apply.

==== Decisions for you
<decisions-for-you>
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-litnotes-titles-apply") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-author-notes-preview") used to return their
  results; they now do so only when
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-async-work-synchronous") is set (the tests
  use it).
- A single huge item (one very large note) still takes its time; slices
  end at item boundaries.

==== Test plan
<test-plan>
- ☒ ERT on Emacs 29.3: new
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "tests/sp-async-work-test.el") (10: stages, a
  probe timer runs between slices, cancel and cleanup, error, one job
  per name, waits for the minibuffer, full titles apply and refs convert
  from timers, a note edited during the question is skipped, a 450-entry
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".bib") in several slices) and the existing
  author-notes 19, bibtex 4, refs 6, titles 8 (run synchronously).

- ☒ #raw(lang:"verbatim", "tools/check-tangle.sh"): only the four edited
  modules differ from HEAD, as expected; tangled output committed.

- ☐ Not run: the real vault. Try
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "M-x sp-litnotes-titles-report") and keep typing
  while it reads.

- Note: #raw(lang:"verbatim", "tests/sp-org-lint-test.el") fails with a
  void #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-var-dir") also on \#228, unrelated.

