==== Summary
<summary>
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "M-x sp-author-notes-bibtex") made Emacs
unresponsive. It was not a missing file: every author note was compared
with every author of every entry (people × entries × authors, each
comparison allocating strings). With \#229\'s slices it still froze,
because one author note is one indivisible step and took seconds on a
big #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".bib").

- The entries without a litnote are indexed once (new \"indexing\"
  stage) by full author name and by last name plus first initial; a note
  looks up only its own keys. Results and their order are unchanged.
- Missing files: a file of #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-bibtex-files") that
  cannot be read is skipped with a message. No files, or none readable,
  stops with a #raw(lang:"verbatim", "user-error") naming them and
  suggesting #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-bibtex-register").
- Doc (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-org-lint.org")) tangled, CHANGELOG
  entry added.

==== Measurements (synthetic vault, Emacs 29.3)
<measurements-synthetic-vault-emacs-293>
- 3,000 author notes, 500 entries: 48.7 s before.
- 3,000 author notes, 40,000 entries (31 MB
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".bib")), 5,000 litnotes: about 7 s after (3.8 s
  reading the #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".bib")), in slices. Before,
  extrapolated, hours.

