==== What
<what>
CUA is gone on every system, and the Emacs defaults for the same jobs
are unbound, so there is one layer of keys.

- #strong[Mac (Command = Super):] #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-c") copy,
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-x") cut, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-v") paste,
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-z") undo, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-Z") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "S-s-z") redo (the latter is the event Emacs
  usually gets for Command+Shift+Z).
- #strong[Linux and Windows (Ctrl):] #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-S-c")
  copy, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-S-x") cut, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-v")
  paste, #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-z") undo,
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-S-z") redo.
- #strong[Unbound everywhere:] #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-y"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-w"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "M-w"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-_"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-/"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-?"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-M-_"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-x u").
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-text-manipulation.org") documents it,
  including a caveats list for unbinding
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-w=/=M-w=/=C-y").
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-keys.org") no longer says CUA is on. The
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".el") is the tangled copy.

