Follow-up to \#294.

==== What
<what>
#strong[Emacs (Linux/Windows,
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-text-manipulation"))]

- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-c") is translated to
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-c") and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-x") to
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-x")
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "key-translation-map")), so Super carries the
  prefixes: #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-c C-c") =
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-c C-c"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "s-x C-f") =
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-x C-f").
- A typed #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-c") / #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-x")
  that starts a key sequence becomes copy / cut (private events
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{3c}sp-copy>"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{3c}sp-cut>")). Inside a sequence they keep their
  stock meaning. This replaces the #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-S-c") /
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-S-x") from \#294.
- On Windows #raw(lang:"verbatim", "w32-lwindow-modifier") /
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "w32-rwindow-modifier") are
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "super").
- Mac is unchanged.

#strong[i3 and sway (options for freeing Super)]

- Chosen: Caps Lock becomes the Level 3 shift
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "lv3:caps_switch"), Mod5) and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{24}mod") is #raw(lang:"verbatim", "Mod5").
  Nothing binds Mod4 any more, so the Windows key reaches Emacs. Same
  physical layer key as AeroSpace on the Mac.
- Other options, not taken: #raw(lang:"verbatim", "caps:hyper") (Hyper
  often shares Mod4 in X, so it may not free Super); Alt as
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{24}mod") (clashes with Meta, which Sia reserved
  for Emacs); Ctrl+Alt chords (awkward, and Ctrl+Alt is used by
  terminals/Emacs).
- Caveat of the choice: Caps+letter yields AltGr-level characters on
  layouts that have them, but the WM grabs bound combinations first.

#strong[Docs]

- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-keys.org"): new section \"Replaced prefixes
  and chords\" with a table of stock key vs Mac vs Linux/Windows, and
  how to look up a key (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-h k"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-h w")).
- Top-level #raw(lang:"verbatim", "README.org"): note that most keys are
  bound differently, pointing to that table.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-text-manipulation.org") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "global-keymap-namespaces.org") updated, with
  the caveats (Windows takes #raw(lang:"verbatim", "Win+C") /
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "Win+X")\; needs a remap).

