== Description
<description-321>
#emph[Requested by #strong[Sia] ·
#link("https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01Xb5LqaNLG6saSgoKaUBss1?thread=cmsg_01Xb5LqaNLG6saSgoKaUBss1U4FeuYXuDT9CPaA3fvY258")[project thread]]

==== Why
<why>
The #raw(lang:"verbatim", "literature") database (about 65 GB) is too
big for the iPad and for the NAS\'s WebDAV sync store, which has about
20 GB on the SSD. So #raw(lang:"verbatim", "literature") stays on the
Mac, searchable from DEVONthink and Emacs, and a small
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "reading") database syncs instead. A rule fills
it, so you don\'t sort anything by hand.

==== What
<what>
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "tools/devonthink-reading.py") has two actions.
Both are dry runs unless you pass #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--apply").

- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "create") makes
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "~/Databases/reading.dtBase2") with one group
  per selected #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".bib") file. If the file exists
  but is closed, it opens it instead.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "send") moves a PDF into
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "reading") when its litnote\'s
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "read-status") is one of the chosen values
  (default #raw(lang:"verbatim", "to-be-read")) and its citekey is in
  one of the chosen #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".bib") files (default: all of
  them). It refuses to go over the budget (default 20 GB). With
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--return"), it moves records that no longer
  match back to their old group, which it remembers in
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "~/.local/state/devonthink-reading/origins.json").
- You can change the rule as you go: #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--status")
  and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--bib") (both repeatable), or
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "~/.config/devonthink-reading/config.json").
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--json") prints the plan as JSON for a later
  Emacs command.
- The rule matches on the citekey: #raw(lang:"verbatim", "ROAM_REFS") or
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "#+reference:") in the litnote, the
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".bib") entry key, and the PDF file name
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "KEY.pdf").
- The script and its tests are literate: they tangle from
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "docs/tangles/academic/devonthink-reading.org"),
  which also has the usage, the settings, how the rule decides, and what
  is untested. Edit the #raw(lang:"verbatim", ".org") and tangle it
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "C-c C-v t"));
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "tools/tangle-all.el") and the
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "tangle") CI check only cover
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "sp-*.org") module docs, so they don\'t check
  this one.
- Also summarised in
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "docs/architecture/devonthink-emacs-integration.org"),
  section \"The #raw(lang:"verbatim", "reading") database\".

==== Tested
<tested>
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "python3 -m unittest tests/test_devonthink_reading.py"):
  11 tests pass. They cover parsing, the rule, the budget,
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--return"), dry run against
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--apply"), and remembering where each record
  came from, with DEVONthink faked.
- #strong[Not run on the Mac.] The DEVONthink calls are untested, and so
  is the question of whether moving an indexed record between databases
  leaves its PDF in #raw(lang:"verbatim", "~/Documents/literature/").
  Try #raw(lang:"verbatim", "create"), then
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "send --apply --limit 1"), and check the record
  and the file.

