== Description
<description-338>
Follows \#340. #raw(lang:"verbatim", "typst compile") on the PDF from
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "gh-pr-pdf") failed on a pull request whose title
contains #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{40}key"):

```
error: label `<key>` does not exist in the document
= PR \#190: ✨ Show the reference of @key links on hover
```

Typst reads #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{40}name") in text as a reference, and
the same happened on the matching commit line. The first version relied
on #raw(lang:"verbatim", "pandoc") escaping it, which it does in 3.1.3
(the version in the container, where I could not reproduce the error)
but evidently not in the version used for the failing run.

==== Changes
<changes>
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "gh-pr-pdf/.local/bin/gh-pr-pdf"):
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{40}"), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{24}") and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{3c}") in pull request text are swapped for
  placeholder characters before #raw(lang:"verbatim", "pandoc") sees
  them and turned into #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\\u{40}"),
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\\u{24}") and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\\u{3c}")
  in the Typst source afterwards. That form is valid in running text and
  inside string arguments such as link targets, so it does not depend on
  how a given #raw(lang:"verbatim", "pandoc") version escapes. Fenced
  code blocks and inline code keep the real characters.
  - #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{24}...\u{24}") is the same kind of clash (Typst
    math), and #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{3c}key>") in text is a label.
- Same file: the Markdown stage no longer reads
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{24}...\u{24}") as math
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "tex_math_dollars") off). Before, a plain
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "\u{24}HOME/\u{24}PATH") in a pull request body was
