== Description
<description-347>
Follows \#340 and \#341. #raw(lang:"verbatim", "gh-pr-pdf") fetched
every pull request again on every run. After the first run it now asks
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "github-backup") for only what changed.

==== Changes
<changes>
All in #raw(lang:"verbatim", "gh-pr-pdf/") (script and
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "README.org"), which has a new \"Running it
again\" section).

- Fetching: the script always passes
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "github-backup -i"). The first run has no
  checkpoints, so it fetches everything and
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "github-backup") writes them. Later runs request
  only new data since the last successful backup.
- #raw(lang:"verbatim", "backup/.gh-pr-pdf-state.json") records the last
  full fetch, the options it used and a hash per rendered pull request.
  A failed fetch does not move it forward.
- A full fetch is forced:
  - when no earlier one is recorded, or with
    #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--full")\;
  - when the options differ from the last run, because
    #raw(lang:"verbatim", "-i") does not backfill a flag that is new;
  - when the last full fetch is older than
    #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--full-every DAYS") (30 by default, 0 turns
    it off). The #raw(lang:"verbatim", "github-backup") documentation
    warns that a failed earlier run can leave a gap in an incremental
    backup; this is the cure.
  - A full fetch first deletes #raw(lang:"verbatim", "github-backup")\'s
    own checkpoint files (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "last_update"),
    #raw(lang:"verbatim", "reviews_last_update")) in
    #raw(lang:"verbatim", "backup/"), so it is not cut short by an older
    checkpoint.
- Rendering: an org file is rebuilt only when the JSON of its pull
  request changed (or the renderer did), since
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "pandoc") runs several times per pull request.
  Org files of pull requests that vanish from the backup are removed.
  The PDF is still built from all the org files, a local step that does
  not use the API.
- Discussions stay a full fetch each time (one query per 25
  discussions).
- The progress line says which mode ran and why, for example
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "fetching pull requests: incremental (last full fetch 3 days ago, ...)").

==== Not run
<not-run>
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "github-backup") is not installed in the container
and there is no way to install it, so the incremental mode was run
against a stand-in that logs how it is called and writes checkpoint
files the way its documentation describes. Covered: first run, an
unchanged rerun (0 rendered, 3 unchanged), one changed pull request (1
rendered), #raw(lang:"verbatim", "--full") (checkpoints deleted first),
changed options, a 40-day-old state against 29 days,
#raw(lang:"verbatim", "--full-every 0"), a failed fetch (state
unchanged), a pull request that vanishes, and a corrupt state file.

Not seen, and worth checking on a real run:

- How #raw(lang:"verbatim", "github-backup") applies
  #raw(lang:"verbatim", "-i") to pull requests, and whether it fetches
  the comments and commits of one that changed (the PyPI page only says
  that it requests new data since the last successful backup).
- That it fetches everything when it finds no checkpoint, which the
  first run and every full fetch rely on.
- Quick check: compare the API requests of the second run with the first
  (#raw(lang:"verbatim", "gh api rate_limit") before and after).

#raw(lang:"verbatim", "typst compile") is still not run in the container
either (see \#341).

